  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1254 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nSunday morning is showing higher pops, but the overall soundings do \nindicate that it may be some low clouds over the Panhandles and \nperhaps just some drizzle with a few pockets of showers embedded. By \nlate morning we may have some clearing and thats where scattered \nshowers/storms will be in play.  But it looks to be later in the \nevening now on Sunday when the more expansive cover of \nshowers/storms will occur.  Latest NBM probs of over an inch really \nstart to drop off, while still on the higher side, outside of the \nnorthwest Panhandles.  And updated deterministic amounts outside of \nthe northwest Panhandles for this event are more in the 0.25-0.5\" \nrange.  Not to say that areas couldn't get over an inch, but it's \nnot as favored as previous forecasts.  We'll still keep high chances \nof precipitation going through Monday and have it drop off into \nTuesday morning.  Temperatures will be a bit cooler on Tuesday with \nhighs in the 60s to near 70, and then we should be dry and back in \nthe 70s by Wednesday.  Thursday we'll keep an eye on as there's \npotentially another weather system coming in from the southwest that \ncould bring out next chance of showers and storms to at least the \nsouthern Panhandles. \n\nWeber",
